{
  "dom_id": "step-q213274",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}a≡{{field-2}}b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,{{field-3}})\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=3,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 3a≡3\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3a≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5)\\,⟺\\,3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
